content:
The v6 Reactor provides `RelationalDbProcessor<DB>` as a base class for processors that need database access. Key features: `getNamespace(driveId)` static method creates per-drive database isolation preventing data collisions; `initAndUpgrade()` lifecycle hook runs migrations on startup; `this.relationalDb` provides a typed Kysely instance; `query(driveId, relationalDb)` static method enables querying from subgraphs. The factory creates namespaced stores via `module.relationalDb.createNamespace<T>(namespace)`. Scaffolding is generated with `ph generate processor --name <name> --type relationalDb --document-types <type>`.

_Corrected 2026-08-13 against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._

## Correction at v6.2.2-dev.74
"Lifecycle management" overstates what the base class does about migrations. At v6.2.2-dev.74 the generated template makes migrations the processor's own responsibility:

```ts
export class MyProcessor extends RelationalDbProcessor<DB> {
  override async initAndUpgrade(): Promise<void> { await up(this.relationalDb); }
}
```

So there is no automatic discovery of a migrations folder — `initAndUpgrade` is the hook, and the processor calls its own `up`. Per-drive namespace isolation (`static getNamespace(driveId)`, defaulting to `${this.name}_${driveId.replaceAll("-","_")}`) and the type-safe Kysely surface are unchanged.
